detail.abstract:
Customer loyalty programs have become an important strategic tool for retailers seeking to retain customers and enhance long-term revenue. In the highly competitive grocery retail sector, where purchase frequency is high and switching costs are low, understanding the effectiveness of loyalty programs in influencing sales revenue is critical. This study examines the impact of customer loyalty programs on sales revenue among grocery retail consumers.&nbsp;
<div>
 The study adopts a quantitative research approach using primary data collected through a structured questionnaire administered to grocery retail consumers. A total of 252 responses were collected, and data from loyalty program members were used for statistical analysis. Customer loyalty was treated as the key independent variable, while sales revenue impact was considered the dependent variable. Correlation and multiple regression analyses were employed to examine the relationships among the study variables.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings reveal that customer loyalty has a significant positive impact on sales revenue, indicating that loyalty programs are effective when they foster customer preference, emotional connection, and repeat purchase behavior. In contrast, membership duration and frequency of loyalty benefit usage do not show a significant direct impact on sales revenue. Additionally, average bill size exhibits a negative relationship with perceived revenue impact, suggesting that loyalty programs may be less influential for high-spending customers.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The study concludes that customer loyalty programs contribute to sales revenue primarily by strengthening customer loyalty rather than through program participation metrics alone. The findings offer valuable managerial insights for grocery retailers, emphasizing the need to design loyalty programs that focus on long-term customer engagement and relationship building to achieve sustainable revenue growth.
</div>
